6.4 Exercise: what determines complexity?
The second exercise asks us to reason about complexity. Termination alone does not give a useful time bound.
The main cost issues in these particular rules are:

traversing terms to find an occurrence of a variable;
performing the occurs check;
propagating a binding through other equations;
representing and copying terms introduced by substitution;
producing the final substitution in the required output representation.

With literal tree copying, a term substituted into several occurrences is duplicated. Repeated propagation can therefore make the expanded representation exponentially larger than the compact input, even though only finitely many rewrites occur. Writing out a fully expanded answer can itself be expensive.
With shared term structures, identical subterms need not be copied repeatedly. The size of a shared representation can be much smaller than the size of the corresponding fully expanded tree. Consequently, a complexity discussion must state whether it measures expanded terms or shared structures, and which operations are taken as primitive.
The six rules alone do not specify enough implementation detail to justify one exact running-time bound. The exercise’s central lesson is to separate termination, number of rewrites, cost per rewrite, and size of the represented answer.

8.4 How to verify the exercises yourself
Load each exercise as a separate program, then issue its query. Keeping them separate avoids accidentally combining predicates named p from different exercises. Ask for further answers in Exercise 2 to observe backtracking.
When comparing an interpreter result with the derivations, check:

whether the query succeeds or fails;
the bindings of the original query variables;
whether more answers exist;
whether a trace rejects a clause head or enters its body and later fails;
whether differences are merely fresh variable names.

For Exercise 1, expect success with no query-variable bindings. For Exercise 2, expect the three bindings just derived. For Exercise 3, expect A = s(0). Exact prompt formatting and the names of internal variables depend on the interpreter.

2.2 Why SAT is useful
SAT was the first problem proved NP-complete. Consequently, every problem in NP can be transformed into SAT by a polynomial-time reduction. A finite-domain CSP is typically in NP, so it can in principle be encoded as a SAT instance and solved using software such as MiniSAT, PicoSAT, RelSAT, or another competition-grade SAT solver.
The theoretical existence of a reduction does not make the encoding automatic. The modeller must decide:

which Boolean variables represent the original choices;
which clauses enforce the original constraints;
how original structured variables are mapped to the integer identifiers required by a solver;
how a Boolean model is translated back into a solution of the initial problem.

This lecture moves from MiniZinc syntax and search annotations to the modelling of complete problems. The central workflow is:

start from a real problem;
identify the unknown quantities;
represent those quantities with decision variables;
assign a domain to every decision variable;
express the relationships among the variables as constraints;
choose satisfaction or optimisation;
leave the solving stage to MiniZinc and its back-end solver, initially using built-in search heuristics.







flowchart LR
    P["Real problem"] --&gt; V["Decision variables"]
    V --&gt; D["Domains"]
    D --&gt; C["Constraints"]
    C --&gt; G["Solve goal"]
    G --&gt; S["MiniZinc solver"]
    S --&gt; O["Solution or infeasibility"]


 The modelling workflow used throughout the lecture. 



The modelling stage and the solving stage are intentionally separated. The model states what a solution must satisfy. The solver decides how to obtain one through propagation, search, and heuristics.






ImportantModel before tuning search



A search annotation cannot repair an incorrect model. Variables, domains, and constraints must first encode the intended problem faithfully. Search choices become relevant only after the model is logically correct.

3.1 Pairwise formulation
The professor’s first formulation is:
par int: n = 100;
array[1..n] of var 1..n: q;

constraint
  forall(i, j in 1..n where i &lt; j)(
    q[i] != q[j] /\
    q[i] + i != q[j] + j /\
    q[i] - i != q[j] - j
  );

solve satisfy;
For every pair of columns \(i&lt;j\):

q[i] != q[j] prevents the queens from sharing a row;
q[i] + i != q[j] + j prevents one diagonal direction;
q[i] - i != q[j] - j prevents the other diagonal direction.

The diagonal conditions are equivalent to
\[
|q_i-q_j|\ne j-i,
\]
but the two linear expressions avoid abs and separate the two diagonal families explicitly.
The output block reconstructs the board:
output [
  if fix(q[j]) == i then " Q " else " . " endif ++
  if j == n then "\n" endif
  | i, j in 1..n
];
fix(q[j]) reads the solved value of the decision variable. A Q is printed exactly when row i equals the solved row of the queen in column j.

3.2 Global-constraint formulation
The second formulation uses all_different:
include "globals.mzn";

int: n = 50;
array[1..n] of var 1..n: q;

constraint all_different(q);
constraint all_different(i in 1..n)(q[i] + i);
constraint all_different(i in 1..n)(q[i] - i);

solve satisfy;
output [show(q)];
The three global constraints correspond exactly to rows and the two diagonal directions.








