5.1 Parameters and decision variables
Every MiniZinc declaration has a type. The lecture distinguishes parameters from decision variables:
par int: a = 3;
var int: b;
A parameter is fixed for one model instance and should be assigned as soon as possible. par is the default qualification. A var declaration introduces a value that the solver must determine and should therefore be written explicitly.
The principal types introduced in the deck are:

int, for integer values and finite-domain decision variables;
bool, for Boolean values, which are a special finite-domain case;
float, for floating-point variables in hybrid models;
string, additionally available for textual values.

A decision variable should normally have a specified domain:
var 0..100: v;
var {0, 2, 4, 6}: w;
The first declaration uses an integer interval, written with two dots. The second explicitly lists the allowed values.






ImportantParameters are fixed; decision variables are searched



A parameter such as a receives one value for the model instance. A decision variable such as b, v, or w represents an unknown whose value must be restricted by constraints and eventually determined by solving.

5.2 Arrays
MiniZinc supports arrays with one or more index sets:
array[indexset1, indexset2] of var type: varname;
The examples from the lecture are:
array[0..2] of var 1..5: v;
array[1..5, 1..5] of var 0..2: M;
The first declaration creates a one-dimensional array indexed by \(0,1,2\). The second creates a \(5\times5\) array. Elements are accessed as v[i] and M[i,j].

5.3 Set variables and set expressions
A declaration such as
set of 1..8: s;
allows s to denote a subset of \(\{1,\ldots,8\}\). The deck lists operations for constructing set expressions:

membership: in;
inclusion: subset, superset;
union: union;
intersection: inter;
difference: diff;
symmetric difference: symdiff;
cardinality: card.

6.1 Universal constraints and comprehensions
A family of pairwise constraints can be generated through forall. The list-comprehension form shown in the deck is:
constraint forall([
  v[i] != v[j] |
  i, j in 1..3 where i &lt; j
]);
A more readable generator form is:
constraint forall(i, j in 1..3 where i &lt; j)(
  v[i] != v[j]
);
The indices range over 1..3, while where i &lt; j removes duplicate and reflexive pairs. The body then requires every selected pair of variables to have different values.

2.2 Minimally adequate teachers and black-box learning
The idealised interface is called a minimally adequate teacher (MAT). Exact equivalence queries can be unrealistic. If the teacher already possesses the complete target automaton, directly transferring it would be simpler than testing hypotheses. In black-box learning, equivalence is approximated through experiments, test suites, or repeated membership queries. The current hypothesis is retained while tested behaviours agree with the black box.
This substitution is practical but weakens the guarantee. Passing a finite test suite establishes agreement only on that suite, not language equivalence. A missed behaviour can produce a later counterexample.
The parking-machine illustration uses inputs such as \(5\,\text{¢}\), \(10\,\text{¢}\), and the production of a parking ticket. By submitting sequences of inputs and observing outputs or acceptance, the learner reconstructs a behavioural model without inspecting the implementation. The applications named in the deck are:

system identification and diagram inference in control theory;
model learning for verification, in contrast with checking an already supplied model;
grammar inference and regular extrapolation;
protocol-state fuzzing in security.

The propositional model-checking problem receives a formula \(\varphi\) and a valuation \(S\) and asks whether \(S\models\varphi\). The algorithm evaluates the syntax tree recursively. At a leaf it reads \(S(p)\); at a connective it recursively evaluates the immediate subformulas and combines their truth values.
Model-check(φ, S):
  if φ = p, return S(p)
  if φ = φ₁ ∨ φ₂, return Model-check(φ₁,S) or Model-check(φ₂,S)
  if φ = φ₁ ∧ φ₂, return Model-check(φ₁,S) and Model-check(φ₂,S)
  if φ = ¬φ₁, return not Model-check(φ₁,S)
  treat → and ↔ according to their Boolean semantics
The running time is polynomial, in fact linear in the size of the represented formula when each node is visited once. The deck records the sharper complexity statement that propositional model checking is P-complete.

A network is built from automata whose ordinary locations and edges are enhanced with:

bounded-range variables, such as int[0,100] i;
real-valued clocks, such as clock x;
shared memory, such as bool flag;
synchronous send and receive actions, such as ack! and ack?.

A channel send and its matching receive occur together. In the introductory example, one component emits inc!, another receives inc?, and the receiver updates count++. The same pattern applies to dec! and dec?.

A global state records:

one current location for every automaton;
values of local and global variables;
values of all clocks;
satisfaction of the relevant invariants.

The state is therefore not just a location name. Two executions can occupy the same tuple of locations but represent different global states because variables or clock values differ.

3.2 Algorithm 1: flags only
Each process sets its own flag before waiting:
flags[pid] := true;
while (flags[1-pid] == true) {}
On exit, it clears the flag. The model satisfies mutual exclusion because a process enters the critical location only when the other flag is false. It is not deadlock-free: both processes can set their flags to true, after which each waits for the other to clear its flag. Neither process can proceed to the critical location and neither can reach its clearing operation.

3.3 Algorithm 2: victim only
The second proposal uses one shared variable:
victim := pid;
while (victim == pid) {}
The model still verifies mutual exclusion, but it is not deadlock-free. A lone process can write its own identifier to victim and then wait forever because no competing process updates the variable. The algorithm blocks even without contention.

2.1 Failures and the motivation for formal methods
The logic deck motivates formal reasoning through costly failures:

the 1994 Pentium floating-point division bug, associated in the slides with losses exceeding 475 million US dollars for Intel;
the 1996 Ariane 5 overflow failure, associated with losses exceeding 370 million;
the 2016 DAO smart-contract attack, associated with losses exceeding 50 million US dollars for Ethereum users and with a subsequent fork.

The point is not that logic automatically prevents every defect. Rather, informal confidence, testing alone, or unchecked assumptions may fail to cover rare numerical cases, unexpected state combinations, or adversarial behaviours. A formal model makes the relevant behaviours explicit, while a formal specification states exactly what must hold. Verification asks whether all behaviours represented by the model satisfy that specification.

The lecture represents entropy with overlapping regions:

the entire \(X\) region has size \(H(X)\);
the entire \(Y\) region has size \(H(Y)\);
the overlap is \(I(X;Y)\);
the part of \(X\) outside the overlap is \(H(X\mid Y)\);
the part of \(Y\) outside the overlap is \(H(Y\mid X)\);
the union of both regions is \(H(X,Y)\).

The diagram is an intuition aid. The equalities above, rather than geometric area, are the formal definitions.

2.4 Decompression
Decompression applies the inverse interpretation of each triple:

if \(o=l=0\), append the literal symbol \(s\);
otherwise, move \(o\) positions backwards in the already reconstructed output;
copy \(l\) symbols from that position;
append \(s\);
advance to the next tuple.

The decoder can reconstruct the same search buffer from the output already produced. Therefore, the dictionary does not need to be transmitted separately.

5.2 Brief historical development
The lecture highlights several milestones:

Sketchpad, developed by Ivan Sutherland in 1963;
the notion of a Constraint Satisfaction Problem, associated with Ugo Montanari in 1972;
domain filtering, associated with David Waltz in 1975;
constraint propagation research in the late 1970s and early 1980s;
Constraint Logic Programming in the late 1980s;
global constraints and additional languages during the 1990s;
hybrid methods, standardisation, parallelism, and learning in the new millennium;
the MiniZinc Challenge from 2010 onward.

The historical message is that modern Constraint Programming combines modelling languages, inference algorithms, search techniques, and optimisation methods developed over several decades.

10.2 Partial assignments, early checks, and backtracking
A better method tests constraints as soon as enough variables have been assigned to evaluate them.
The procedure is:

assign a value to the next variable;
check every constraint whose involved variables are assigned;
if a violation is detected, abandon the current branch immediately;
otherwise continue with the next variable;
when no value remains, backtrack to the previous choice.

For the illustrated 4-Queens search, early checking and backtracking reduce the exploration to 19 attempts rather than 256 complete assignments.
Assignments beginning with \(X_1=3\) or \(X_1=4\) are symmetric to already visited cases. This introduces symmetry breaking: if two regions of the search space are equivalent under a symmetry of the problem, one representative region may be explored while the equivalent one is excluded.
Even backtracking with partial checks remains insufficient for many medium and large instances. The exercise proposed in the lecture is to implement both naive methods for the \(n\)-Queens problem and determine the largest practically solvable \(n\).

13.3 Feasibility versus proving optimality
CSP and COP are closely related in principle, but may behave differently in practice:

a large CSP may be solved quickly if a feasible assignment is found early;
proving that a COP solution is globally optimal may require exploring a large part of the search space;
in some applications, a sufficiently good solution is acceptable even when global optimality is not proved.

8.2 Example of standardisation
Suppose a CSP contains:

one binary constraint on \((X,Y)\);
two binary constraints on \((X,Z)\);
no binary constraint on \((Y,Z)\);
one ternary constraint on \((X,Y,Z)\).

To make the binary part standard:

intersect the two relations concerning \((X,Z)\);
add the universal relation \(D_Y\times D_Z\) for \((Y,Z)\);
leave the ternary constraint unchanged.

Standardisation concerns the organisation of binary relations; it does not replace higher-arity constraints.

8.2 Functions, injections, surjections and bijections
Assume that domain/1 and range/1 are given extensionally and define, respectively, the domain (A) and range (B) of a function. Define a predicate f/2 representing a function from (A) to (B), so that the answer sets return all such functions. Then define predicates for:

inj: a one-to-one (injective) function;
surj: an onto (surjective) function;
bij: a bijection, if one exists.

The slide notes that != is a built-in operator that may be used.

7.1 Old MiniZinc support
The slides document an old MiniZinc LNS interface:
int: iterations = 100;
float: destructionRate = 0.3;
int: time_ms = 5 * 1000;

solve search
  lns_min(obj, x, iterations, destructionRate, time_ms);
Here x is the decision-variable list and obj stores the objective. The lecture explicitly states that this library is no longer maintained. The syntax is historical course material, not a recommendation that the interface remains available.
The slides also mention solver-independent LNS work, a constraint-based local-search backend, CLP implementations, and an ad-hoc GPU solver used for protein-structure prediction.
